{
  "dom_id": "step-e12695",
  "name": "Example: Negating a Sentence Containing a Disjunction",
  "problem": "What is the negation of the following compound statement?\n\n$\\qquad$ $3$ is not prime or $\\pi$ is not rational.",
  "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\lor B) = \\neg A \\land \\neg B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(3\\text{\u00a0is not prime}\\text{\u00a0or\u00a0}\\pi \\text{\u00a0is not rational}) & \u2261\u00ac((3\\text{\u00a0is not prime})\u2228(\\pi \\text{\u00a0is not rational})) \\\\  & \u2261\u00ac(3\\text{\u00a0is not prime})\u2227\u00ac(\\pi \\text{\u00a0is not rational}) \\\\  & \u2261(3\\text{\u00a0is prime})\u2227(\\pi \\text{\u00a0is rational}) \\\\  & \u22613\\text{\u00a0is prime}\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}\\pi \\text{\u00a0is rational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
